  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a sharp decline (e.g., from ~77 to ~67) followed by low sentiment scores (0.00-0.20), the model systematically underestimates the immediate rebound magnitude, often failing to capture the full volatility of the subsequent 30-day spike.",
    "The model exhibits a strong mean-reversion bias in its forecasts; when true outcomes show a rapid rise followed by an equally rapid fall (V-shape or inverted V), the model's forecast remains flat or slowly rising, missing the peak and the subsequent crash.",
    "In scenarios where sentiment is near zero but price action indicates high volatility (large daily swings), the model fails to adjust its 30-day horizon forecast, resulting in a persistent underestimation of downside risk after a spike.",
    "The model consistently ignores the 'momentum reversal' signal present in the true outcomes; while prices often surge immediately after a low point, the model predicts a continuation of the downtrend or a very slow recovery.",
    "For 30-day horizons following a period of consolidation at lower levels (e.g., 67-68), the model assumes a gradual upward drift that is frequently invalidated by sudden, high-amplitude corrections or crashes in the true data."
  ]
}
